## (B) Printed equations and source-defined symbols

Mathematical transcriptions retain source numbers, signs and factors; LaTeX serialization/layout is normalized. Unnumbered formulas are labeled. An author's characterization of a condition is reported, not independently endorsed.

### S1 — published KS equations, cross-checked with arXiv v2

S1 uses Kantowski–Sachs, not Bianchi I. Eq. (18) defines a directional bounce; adjacent text extends the definition to X and a and distinguishes the three bounce times. Eq. (32) is the author's singularity-avoidance inequality, not a common turning-point equation.

Definitions: X(t), Y(t) directional scale factors; a volume scale factor; H mean Hubble parameter; dot=d/dt; c=1, κ=8πG. ε and p are thermodynamic density and pressure; n_f fermion number density; α=κ(ℏc)²/32. Tildes denote effective totals. u is four-velocity, θ expansion, σ² shear, ω² rotation, w four-acceleration; semicolon means Levi-Civita derivative. Comoving KS has zero rotation and acceleration. The s in dθ/ds is the worldline parameter, distinct from entropy density s elsewhere.

Section 7 prints the following substitution and condition, unnumbered:
\[
-R_{\mu\nu}u^\mu u^\nu=-\kappa(\tilde\epsilon+3\tilde p)/2,\qquad
-\kappa(\tilde\epsilon+3\tilde p)/2>2\sigma^2.
\]
The shear-free relativistic specialization sets σ²=0 in (32); it is not separately numbered or solved as a FLRW collapse in S1.

Equation **(1)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E1):

\[
\tilde{\epsilon}=\epsilon-\alpha n_{\textrm{f}}^{2},\quad\tilde{p}=p-\alpha n_{\textrm{f}}^{2},
\tag{1}
\]

Equation **(6)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E6):

\[
ds^{2}=dt^{2}-X^{2}(t)dr^{2}-Y^{2}(t)(d\vartheta^{2}+\sin^{2}\vartheta\,d\varphi^{2}).
\tag{6}
\]

Equation **(9)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E9); all three printed rows:
\[
\begin{aligned}
R^0_0&=-\frac{\ddot X}{X}-\frac{2\ddot Y}{Y}
=\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde\epsilon+3\tilde p),\\
R^1_1&=-\frac{\ddot X}{X}-\frac{2\dot X\dot Y}{XY}
=\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde p-\tilde\epsilon),\\
R^2_2=R^3_3&=-\frac{\ddot Y}{Y}-\frac{\dot X\dot Y}{XY}
-\frac{\dot Y^2}{Y^2}-\frac1{Y^2}
=\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde p-\tilde\epsilon).
\end{aligned}\tag{9}
\]

Equation **(10)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E10); all three printed rows:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\dot Y^2+1}{Y^2}+\frac{2\dot X\dot Y}{XY}&=\kappa\tilde\epsilon,\\
\frac{\dot Y^2+1}{Y^2}+\frac{2\ddot Y}{Y}&=-\kappa\tilde p,\\
\frac{\ddot X}{X}+\frac{\ddot Y}{Y}+\frac{\dot X\dot Y}{XY}&=-\kappa\tilde p.
\end{aligned}\tag{10}
\]

Equation **(11)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E11):

\[
\frac{\ddot{X}}{X}-\frac{\ddot{Y}}{Y}+\frac{\dot{X}\dot{Y}}{XY}-\frac{\dot{Y}^{2}+1}{Y^{2}}=0.
\tag{11}
\]

Equation **(15)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E15):

\[
a=(XY^{2})^{1/3}
\tag{15}
\]

Equation **(16)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E16):

\[
H=\frac{\dot{a}}{a}=\frac{1}{3XY^{2}}\frac{d(XY^{2})}{dt}=\frac{1}{3}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}+\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr)
\tag{16}
\]

Equation **(18)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E18):

\[
\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}=0,\quad\frac{\ddot{Y}}{Y}>0.
\tag{18}
\]

Equation **(23)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E23):

\[
\sigma_{\mu\nu}=u_{(\mu;\nu)}-\frac{1}{3}u^{\rho}_{\phantom{\rho};\rho}(g_{\mu\nu}-u_{\mu}u_{\nu}).
\tag{23}
\]

Equation **(25)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E25):

\[
\sigma^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{\mu}_{\nu}\sigma^{\nu}_{\mu}=\frac{1}{3}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}-\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr)^{2}.
\tag{25}
\]

Equation **(26)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E26):

\[
\theta=u^{\rho}_{\phantom{\rho};\rho}=\Gamma^{\rho}_{\rho\nu}u^{\nu}=\frac{\dot{X}}{X}+\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}=3H.
\tag{26}
\]

Equation **(28)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E28):

\[
\dot{\sigma}^{1}_{1}+3H\sigma^{1}_{1}=\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}.
\tag{28}
\]

Equation **(29)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E29):

\[
\dot{\sigma}^{2}_{2}+3H\sigma^{2}_{2}=\dot{\sigma}^{3}_{3}+3H\sigma^{3}_{3}=-\frac{1}{3Y^{2}}.
\tag{29}
\]

Equation **(30)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E30):

\[
\frac{d(\sigma^{2})}{dt}+6H\sigma^{2}=\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}-\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr).
\tag{30}
\]

Equation **(31)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E31):

\[
\frac{d\theta}{ds}=-\frac{1}{3}\theta^{2}-2(\sigma^{2}-\omega^{2})+w^{\mu}_{\phantom{\mu};\mu}-R_{\mu\nu}u^{\mu}u^{\nu},
\tag{31}
\]

Equation **(32)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E32):

\[
2\kappa\alpha n_{\textrm{f}}^{2}>2\sigma^{2}+\kappa\epsilon.
\tag{32}
\]

Equation **(33)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E33):

\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{-g}}\frac{d(\sqrt{-g}n_{\textrm{f}})}{dt}=\beta H^{4},
\tag{33}
\]

Equation **(34)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E34):

\[
\dot{n}_{\textrm{f}}+3Hn_{\textrm{f}}=\beta H^{4},\quad\frac{d(n^{2}_{\textrm{f}})}{dt}+6Hn^{2}_{\textrm{f}}=2\beta n_{\textrm{f}}H^{4}.
\tag{34}
\]

Equation **(35)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E35):

\[
\frac{2\beta n_{\textrm{f}}}{81}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}+\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr)^{4}>\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}-\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr).
\tag{35}
\]

Equation **(36)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E36):

\[
\frac{\dot{Y}^{2}+1}{Y^{2}}+\frac{2\dot{X}\dot{Y}}{XY}=\kappa(h_{\star}T^{4}-\alpha h_{n\textrm{f}}^{2}T^{6}).
\tag{36}
\]

Equation **(37)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E37):

\[
\frac{\dot{T}}{T}+H=\frac{\beta H^{4}}{3h_{n\textrm{f}}T^{3}}.
\tag{37}
\]

Equation **(38)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11556v2#S0.E38):

\[
\frac{\dot{T}}{T}=H\Bigl(\frac{\beta H^{3}}{3h_{n\textrm{f}}T^{3}}-1\Bigr).
\tag{38}
\]

Production: β is the phenomenological rate parameter. Beside (33), S1 prints g=−X²Y⁴=−a⁶; that expression is retained without inserting an angular factor. Section 9 uses ε=h★T⁴, p=ε/3, n_f=h_nf T³, with
\[
h_\star=\frac{\pi^2}{30}(g_{\rm b}+\tfrac78g_{\rm f})\frac{k_{\rm B}^4}{(\hbar c)^3},
\qquad h_{n{\rm f}}=\frac{\zeta(3)}{\pi^2}\frac34g_{\rm f}\frac{k_{\rm B}^3}{(\hbar c)^3}.
\]
T is temperature, g_b and g_f thermal degrees of freedom, k_B Boltzmann's constant. S1 prints g_b=29, g_f=90. Eq. (35) is its additional production-versus-shear rate inequality, required when (32) is reached after horizon formation so that (32) continues to hold. No numerical β interval is printed.

### S2 — chapter manuscript context; publisher equation numbering unverified

Eq. (34) explicitly chooses r(τ,R)=a(τ)sin R; (30) is the closed isotropic FLRW interval. Eq. (38) is its Friedmann equation; (40) is its two-turning-point existence condition. Section VI defines a turning point by ȧ=0 and a bounce by ä>0 there (unnumbered). This is not S3's cusp prescription.

Definitions: κ=8πG/c⁴; α=κ(ℏc)²/32; ordinary/effective fluid quantities as above. τ synchronous comoving time, R comoving radius, r areal radius, a≥0 scale factor; dot=d/d(cτ), prime=d/dR, H=c ȧ/a. a₀=a(0), T₀=T(0), r₀ initial sphere radius, M its mass, r_g=2GM/c². h★, h_nf and the printed g_b=29, g_f=90 are as above. s_ij is spin density, s²=½s_ij s^ij, Π_i four-momentum density, D the Levi-Civita derivative. G is the Einstein tensor; ε=cΠ_i u^i.

Section VI prints, unnumbered:
\[
\frac{d\theta}{ds}=-\frac{\theta^2}{3}-2\sigma^2-P_{ik}u^iu^k,\qquad
-P_{ik}u^iu^k=-\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde\epsilon+3\tilde p).
\]
P_ik is the Levi-Civita Ricci contraction. The relativistic condition is numbered (41).

Equation **(1)** — [source](https://arxiv.org/html/2307.12190v1#S1.E1):

